{"id":"law","name":"Law","aliases":[],"sources":["nave","easton"],"topics":["nave:law"],"groups":["(Contained in the books of Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers, and Deuteronomy)","To be written","Expounded by"],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Law","text":"A rule of action. (1.) The Law of Nature is the will of God as to human conduct, founded on the moral difference of things, and discoverable by natural light (Rom. 1:20; 2:14, 15). This law binds all men at all times. It is generally designated by the term conscience, or the capacity of being influenced by the moral relations of things. (2.) The Ceremonial Law prescribes under the Old Testament the rites and ceremonies of worship. This law was obligatory only till Christ, of whom these rites were typical, had finished his work (Heb. 7:9, 11; 10:1; Eph. 2:16). It was fulfilled rather than abrogated by the gospel. (3.) The Judicial Law, the law which directed the civil policy of the Hebrew nation. (4.) The Moral Law is the revealed will of God as to human conduct, binding on all men to the end of time. It was promulgated at Sinai. It is perfect (Ps. 19:7), perpetual (Matt. 5:17, 18), holy …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"G3551","lemma":"νόμος","xlit":"nómos","gloss":"law , genitive case , specially","kjv":"law","count":44,"share":0.1},{"strong":"H8451","lemma":"תּוֹרָה","xlit":"tôwrâh","gloss":"a precept or statute, especially the Decalogue or Pentat","kjv":"law","count":38,"share":0.09},{"strong":"H3789","lemma":"כָּתַב","xlit":"kâthab","gloss":"to grave, by implication, to write","kjv":"describe, record, prescribe, subscribe","count":22,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H3871","lemma":"לוּחַ","xlit":"lûwach","gloss":"probably meaning to glisten","kjv":"board, plate, table","count":16,"share":0.04},{"strong":"G1484","lemma":"ἔθνος","xlit":"éthnos","gloss":"a race","kjv":"Gentile, heathen, nation, people","count":18,"share":0.04},{"strong":"G3475","lemma":"Μωσεύς","xlit":"Mōseús","gloss":"Moseus, Moses, or Mouses , the Hebrew lawgiv